SLUG: gallery.html
TITLE: Our work in photographs | Akash Movers, Kanpur
DESC: Photographs of real jobs by Akash Movers in Kanpur - furniture wrapped in corrugated sheet and stretch film, labelled cartons, and consignments staged for loading.
HEADING: Our work, photographed
LEAD: Every photograph on this page is from a job we actually did. Nothing here is stock photography, and nothing has been staged for the website.
CRUMBS: Home|/ ; Gallery|
---

  <section class="section on-paper" aria-labelledby="gallery-title">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">Packed by us</span>
        <h2 id="gallery-title">What careful packing actually looks like</h2>
        <p class="lead">
          Corrugated sheet on the corners and edges, stretch film over the top,
          and a label on every piece before it leaves the floor. These are the
          jobs our crews packed in Kanpur.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="split is-wide-left mb-8">
        <div>
          <h3>What to look for in these photographs</h3>
          <p>
            Packing is mostly invisible work. Once a wardrobe is wrapped it looks
            like every other wrapped wardrobe, and the difference between a job
            that arrives intact and one that does not is in details you only
            notice if someone points them out. So here they are.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Corrugated sheet goes on first, and it goes on the edges.</strong>
            A corner is where a knock lands when a piece is carried down a
            staircase or sits against the side of a vehicle. Several of the
            photographs below show studded edge protection taped along the full
            length of a panel for exactly that reason.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Stretch film goes over the top, by hand.</strong> It holds the
            sheet in place, keeps dust and rain off, and stops drawers and doors
            opening in transit. It is not a substitute for the layer underneath
            it, which is why you can see both in almost every frame.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p>
            <strong>Soft and loose items are strapped into blocks.</strong> Cushions
            and bedding are compressed and banded so they stack squarely instead
            of filling the vehicle with air. A tidier load is a load that moves
            less on the road.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Things get labelled before they leave the floor.</strong> You
            will see handwriting on several of these: which room a piece came
            from, which way up it stands, what is inside. That writing is what
            makes unpacking at the far end take an afternoon rather than a week.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Everything is staged before anything is carried.</strong> The
            shots taken outdoors and in parking areas are consignments gathered
            in loading order, so the vehicle is packed once, properly, instead
            of being repacked three times in the street.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

